## Key Findings

**GPU/Accelerator Orchestration**
- Kubernetes fails to partition GPUs effectively — a cluster of 80GB H100s running small inference jobs can achieve ~5% actual utilization
- Meta's Llama 3 training on 16,384 H100s saw a hardware failure every 3 hours; H100 memory has 3.2x lower MTBE than A100s
- Only 23% of enterprises successfully orchestrate multi-cloud GPU workloads

**Resource Scheduling**
- Real-world GPU utilization: 10–40%; only 7% of orgs exceed 85% at peak
- 60–70% of GPU budget is typically wasted on idle resources
- Up to 65% of training time consumed by data preprocessing, leaving GPUs idle

**Thermal Management**
- NVIDIA Blackwell Ultra TDP: 1,400W per chip (up from ~300W seven years ago)
- Rack densities: 5–10 kW (traditional) → 120–163 kW (Blackwell generation)
- Air cooling fails above ~30 kW/rack; liquid cooling adds $500K–$2M per MW in capex

**Power Consumption**
- Global data center electricity: 415 TWh (2024) → projected 945–980 TWh by 2030 (15%/year growth)
- AI-specific servers growing at 30%/year, projected to quintuple by 2030
- Industry spent $580B on AI data center infrastructure in 2025 alone

**Infrastructure Scaling**
- US grid capacity shortfall: 11 GW now, exceeding 40 GW by 2028
- Grid connection wait times: 4–7 years in major US markets
- Construction cost: $7.7M/MW (2020) → $10.7M/MW (2025); AI fit-out can reach $25M/MW

